    "conceptCards": [
      { "id": "tool_hunter", "title": "Hunter",
        "text": "Hunter reports the address pattern a company uses, for example first initial then surname. A tester reads it to work out how any named employee would be addressed. It learns the pattern from every address ever published: press releases, old PDFs, other firms' contact lists. Defence: keep staff addresses out of what you publish and give outsiders role inboxes such as careers@ledgerwell.example." },
      { "id": "tool_harvester", "title": "theHarvester",
        "text": "theHarvester collects names, email addresses and sub-domains from public sources. The results are noisy: two people can share a name, so check each hit against what the company's own site says before you rely on it." },
      { "id": "tool_recon", "title": "Recon-ng",
        "text": "Recon-ng is a framework that runs many lookups like the ones above from one place. A handle that returns nothing is a result too: it tells you not to spend time on that lead." },
      { "id": "tool_exiftool", "title": "exiftool",
        "text": "exiftool reads the metadata a file carries: author, software, saved-by name and folder path. Staff publish documents without clearing it, and the author field often shows the username convention." },
      { "id": "typo-squat", "title": "Typo-squat",
        "text": "A name one look-alike pair away, such as ledgervvell.example for ledgerwell.example. Read the address letter by letter." },
      { "id": "homograph", "title": "Homograph",
        "text": "A letter from another alphabet that looks the same, so the address reads as ledgerwell.example but is not. Some browsers show the punycode form, xn--, to give it away." },
      { "id": "padlock", "title": "Padlock",
        "text": "A padlock shows the connection to the address is private, not that the site is who it claims to be. A look-alike page gets a padlock just as easily as the real one." },
      { "id": "hover-link", "title": "Hover the link",
        "text": "The words of a link and where it goes are two different things. Hover, and read the address in the status bar, not the text that was typed." },
      { "id": "right-to-left", "title": "Read right to left",
        "text": "Read a web address from the right: the real site is the label just before .example. login.ledgerwell.account-help.example is account-help.example, not ledgerwell.example." },
      { "id": "five-habits", "title": "Five habits",
        "text": "Bookmark the real portal (ledgerwell.example), check the address before you type a password, expect no password request by email, report anything odd, and never trust a padlock alone." }
    ],

